<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second White Terror</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1815">1815</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second White Terror was a wave of royalist reprisals in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1815, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s Hundred Days and the second restoration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xviii">Louis XVIII</a>. Royalist mobs and bands killed Bonapartists, Protestants and former revolutionaries, notably in Marseille, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimes">Nîmes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>, where Marshal Brune was murdered. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> the ultra-royalist Chamber elected that year pressed for purges, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-ney">Marshal Ney</a> was executed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-white-terror.jpg" alt="Second White Terror" width="320" height="332" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gustave Roux (1828-1865) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mort_du_mar%C3%A9chal_Brune_le_2_ao%C3%BBt_1815.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
